---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../../components/UiLabel.astro";
import HelpCard from "../../components/HelpCard.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 4: logging a day — flow, tracked options, tags and
// notes (including the private note), plus editing and deleting. The
// mechanics reuse the in-app help cards; screen names and button labels
// come from the app's ARB strings through <UiLabel>; every factual claim
// carries a `<!-- cite: ... -->` ledger entry checked by
// `site/scripts/check-claims.mjs`.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Logging a day"
  description="How to log a day in lunarlog: flow and spotting, tracked options, tags, and notes — including private notes — plus how editing and deleting work."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Logging a day</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      One tap is enough on an ordinary day; everything else is optional.
      This guide walks a single day's entry — what each part is for, and
      how to change or remove it later.
    </p>
    <section>
      <h2>One day at a time</h2>
      <HelpCard id="log-period" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Starting a day</h2>
      <p>
        When a period starts, logging it is one tap: the overview offers
        <UiLabel key="todayCardLogPeriodStartedToday" />. That records the
        day, marks it as bleeding, and starts a new cycle count —
        history and estimates update from that single tap.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/overview/ (today card quick-log, overviewLoggedSnackbar); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (logPeriod, cycleDay cards) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        Days do not have to be logged the day they happen: the calendar
        opens any past day, so a forgotten day can be filled in later, and
        logging a few periods start-to-finish is what keeps the
        estimates sharp.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (logPeriod, periodLengthAverage cards) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing the app will ask about: starting to log bleeding much
        earlier than expected opens a confirmation —
        <UiLabel key="daySheetCycleStartDialogTitle" /> — because
        logging flow starts a new cycle, while spotting never does. It is
        a checkpoint, not a scolding; either choice keeps the record
        honest.
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (daySheetCycleStartDialog*); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (flowLevels card: spotting is logged separately) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a day holds</h2>
      <p>
        Each day carries one entry with several parts, all optional beyond
        the flow itself:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Flow.</strong> One level per day: <UiLabel
            key="flowLevelNotBleeding"
          />, <UiLabel key="flowLevelLight" />, <UiLabel
            key="flowLevelMedium"
          />, <UiLabel key="flowLevelHeavy" />, or <UiLabel
            key="flowLevelSuperHeavy"
          />. <UiLabel key="flowLevelSpotting" /> is its own tracked
          option, separate from flow — light staining between periods
          belongs there, which keeps period boundaries honest.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (flowLevel*); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (flowLevels card) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tracked options.</strong> Pains, feelings, sleep, and
          other signals, each its own record on the day, each with its own
          detail where it fits. Logging a few consistently matters more
          than logging many occasionally.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (trackedSymptoms card) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tags.</strong> Quick labels that make patterns easy to
          spot later. A day can carry several; the
          <UiLabel key="daySheetCustomTagsLabel" /> list grows with the
          household's own words, searchable from the day sheet.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (daySheetCustomTagsLabel, daySheetTagSearchHint); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (tagsNotes card) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A note.</strong> <UiLabel key="daySheetNoteLabel" /> is
          free text — anything a label cannot hold.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (daySheetNoteLabel); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (tagsNotes card) -->
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The note has one privacy choice, made when it is written: marking
        it private keeps it hers alone. Guardians on the profile see that
        a private note exists — the day shows
        <UiLabel key="daySheetNotePrivateHidden" /> — but not what it
        says.
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (daySheetNotePrivate*); PRIVACY.md#5-minors-privacy--family-profiles (private notes visible only to the subject) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Editing and deleting</h2>
      <HelpCard id="edit-delete-entry" />
      <p>
        To change a day, open it, adjust anything, and
        <UiLabel key="daySheetSave" /> — saving overwrites that day only.
        To remove a day's content, <UiLabel key="daySheetDelete" /> from
        the same place; after confirming
        <UiLabel key="daySheetDeleteTitle" />, the day keeps its place in
        the calendar with nothing logged on it, and a snackbar offers
        <UiLabel key="daySheetUndo" /> for a moment in case the tap was a
        mistake.
        <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (daySheetSave/Delete/DeleteTitle/Undo); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (editDeleteEntry card) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no deadline on corrections: a mis-logged day can be fixed
        at any time, and estimates recompute from the corrected history.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (editDeleteEntry card) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What happens to a logged day</h2>
      <p>
        Everything works offline; entries sync to the account's other
        devices the next time the device is online and signed in. When two
        guardians log on the same day at once, the entries merge rather
        than one overwriting the other — tags combine, and each field
        keeps the newest write.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (logPeriod, offlineMode, syncWhat cards); lib/data/db/storage.dart (same-date conflict resolution) -->
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
